from pathlib import Path
import json,shutil,datetime
import pandas as pd
P=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
O=P.parent/'outputs' if P.name=='work' else P.parent
D=P/'couple_profiles'; F=O/'report-app/src/data.json'
s=json.loads(F.read_text());x=pd.read_csv(D/'complete_couple_profiles.csv');c=pd.read_csv(D/'standardized_direct_contrasts.csv');audit=json.load(open(D/'joint_model_audit.json'))
rows=[]
for z in x.itertuples():
 rows.append(dict(id=z.profile_id,schooling=z.schooling,wages=z.couple_wages,roles=z.earnings_role,rate=round(z.rate_per_1000,2) if pd.notna(z.rate_per_1000) else None,CI=f'{z.ci_low:.2f}–{z.ci_high:.2f}' if pd.notna(z.ci_low) else 'Too few observed events',people=z.respondents,events=z.events,supported=bool(z.supported)))
contrasts=[]
for z in c.iloc[:6].itertuples():contrasts.append(dict(comparison=z.contrast,difference=round(z.difference_per_1000,2),CI=f'{z.difference_ci_low:.2f}–{z.difference_ci_high:.2f}',RR=round(z.rate_ratio,2),ratioCI=f'{z.ratio_ci_low:.2f}–{z.ratio_ci_high:.2f}'))
contrasts=sorted(contrasts,key=lambda r:abs(r['difference']),reverse=True)
source=dict(label='Original joint prospective couple-profile analysis',url='https://nlsinfo.org/cohort-data/nlsy79_all_1979-2022.zip',files=['couple_profiles/analyze_couple_profiles.py','couple_profiles/complete_couple_profiles.csv','couple_profiles/standardized_direct_contrasts.csv','couple_profiles/joint_model_audit.json','couple_profiles/METHODS.md'],filters=['Baseline co-resident opposite-sex marriages, positive combined wages, observed subsequent divorce exposure 2000–2022. Baseline spouse schooling and prior-calendar-year wages precede each interval. Weighted Poisson model, person-time offset and respondent-cluster robust covariance.'],caveats=['Birth cohort 1957–1964; surviving midlife marriages, not future newlyweds.','Not unique couple registry. Proxy spouse reports, topcoding, selection and nonresponse remain.','Main effects control age, current duration, first/subsequent marriage, wave and reporting sex; additive log-rate associations do not establish matching synergy.','Pointwise approximate CIs omit full survey design and standardization-target estimation uncertainty.','Schooling years are not verified graduate credentials. No causal effects or individual forecasts.'])
s['queries']['couple_profiles']={'rows':rows,'source':source};s['queries']['couple_contrasts']={'rows':contrasts,'source':{**source,'label':'Common-scale standardized couple contrasts'}}
low=x[x.supported].sort_values('rate_per_1000').iloc[0];both=x[(x.schooling=='Both 16+')&(x.earnings_role=='Balanced 40–60% wife')].iloc[0]
B=[dict(id='couple-goal',type='text',queryId='couple_profiles',text=f"""## Couple pairings associated with lower annual divorce incidence

The primary goal is to identify supported lower-rate **couple profiles**, then compare the size of the measured associations. This new joint analysis uses **{audit['respondents']:,} respondents, {audit['events']:,} observed divorces and {audit['person_years']:,.0f} person-years**, with both partners' schooling and earnings measured before follow-up. Every adjusted profile uses the same weighted age, marriage-duration, first-versus-subsequent-marriage, calendar-wave and respondent-sex target.

Among the supported combinations tested, **both spouses with 16+ years of schooling, upper-quartile combined wages and both earning with the husband contributing more than 60%** had the lowest model estimate: **{low.rate_per_1000:.2f} divorces per 1,000 married person-years (approximate 95% CI {low.ci_low:.2f}–{low.ci_high:.2f})**. The corresponding balanced-earner profile was **{both.rate_per_1000:.2f} ({both.ci_low:.2f}–{both.ci_high:.2f})**. These are exploratory, model-dependent profiles in an older U.S. cohort, not a statistically proven global optimum.

The data do **not** establish that zero wages for the wife improves this profile. Its matching high-schooling/high-combined-wage cell contains only four observed divorces, so its adjusted profile estimate is withheld. The broader husband-only versus balanced comparison below is uncertain. The earlier crude high-husband-wage comparison likewise did not establish an advantage over two high earners."""),dict(id='couple-profile-table',type='table',queryId='couple_profiles',title='Joint couple profiles: standardized divorces per 1,000 married person-years',columns=[dict(key=k,label=l) for k,l in [('id','Profile'),('schooling','Completed schooling'),('wages','Combined wages'),('roles','Earnings split'),('rate','Per 1,000 years'),('CI','Approx. 95% CI'),('people','People'),('events','Divorces')]]),dict(id='couple-support',type='text',queryId='couple_profiles',text="""### What qualifies as a supported profile?

These nine illustrative archetypes were chosen for the stated questions rather than by searching every possible trait combination for a minimum. Estimates are displayed only with at least **100 distinct respondents and 10 observed divorces** in the matching cell. This exploratory support rule does not guarantee reliable prediction; estimates with 11–19 events remain fragile and borrow strength from the pooled model. Low-event cells remain visible without a modeled ranking.

“Both 12” means 12 completed school years each; “both 16+” pools 16–17 and 18+ years with one fixed observed partner-tier mixture. These are **schooling tiers, not verified bachelor's/master's/PhD credentials**. Q1 and Q4 are within-wave quartiles of **positive combined wage/salary earnings**, not fixed salaries, total household resources or wealth. Both-zero wage couples are outside this model. An annual rate is not a lifetime probability, and multiplying it by marriage length does not produce a valid long-run forecast."""),dict(id='couple-effects-text',type='text',queryId='couple_contrasts',text="""## Which measured differences have the biggest association?

The following comparisons all use **additional or fewer divorces per 1,000 married person-years**, with the other modeled characteristics held to a common distribution. This makes their scale comparable; the chosen changes still differ in size and meaning.

The two-spouse schooling contrast and the husband-majority/both-working contrast show lower annual incidence in this model. The wife-majority point estimate is higher, but its **absolute-difference interval includes zero**; the log-rate-ratio interval differs slightly because the uncertainty transformations use different scales. We lead with absolute differences rather than selecting the more favorable significance result.

**Upper-quartile combined wages alone do not show a clear independent advantage over the lowest quartile once both schooling levels and earnings split are controlled.** This is narrower than the earlier unadjusted high-earner pattern and does not show that raising earnings has no benefit. Husband-only versus balanced is also unresolved. This ranks only the specified contrasts, not every possible cause of divorce."""),dict(id='couple-effects-table',type='table',queryId='couple_contrasts',title='Standardized associations: first group minus reference',columns=[dict(key=k,label=l) for k,l in [('comparison','Comparison'),('difference','Difference /1,000 years'),('CI','Approx. 95% CI'),('RR','Rate ratio'),('ratioCI','Approx. 95% CI')]]),dict(id='couple-limits',type='text',text="""### How to use this profile

The supported descriptive sketch is **two partners with substantial schooling and adequate combined earnings**, with a husband-majority dual-earner configuration worth further replication. The data do not justify requiring a nonworking wife, a particular religion, party or ethnicity as part of an ideal profile. Separate religion, politics and demographic analyses below have different coverage and should not be multiplied into this joint prediction.

These profiles describe marriages already intact and co-resident at baseline, largely in midlife; they do not forecast the outcomes of people choosing partners today. Schooling and income selection, earlier relationship problems, children, health, age gaps, wealth and unmeasured relationship quality can explain differences. Both-zero wages, missing earnings and lost follow-up are excluded. The model does not establish that a particular pairing causes stability. Its additive log-rate structure assumes an earnings-split association shared across schooling groups; repeating it in two profiles is not independent confirmation of a special matching benefit. Read the full methods and independent numerical checks in the download.""")]
B.insert(1,dict(id='couple-profile-figure',type='figure',name='coupleProfiles',queryId='couple_profiles',title='Supported couple profiles on one annual scale',caption='Common-target adjusted rates and approximate respondent-cluster 95% confidence intervals. Both schooling levels, combined wage quartile and earnings split are modeled jointly, controlling age, current marriage duration/order, survey wave and respondent sex. Sparse archetypes are withheld; the model does not establish causal or globally optimal pairings.'))
s['report']['blocks']=B+[b for b in s['report']['blocks'] if not b['id'].startswith('couple-')]
s['generatedAt']=datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).isoformat();s['buildStatus']='complete';F.write_text(json.dumps(s,indent=2,ensure_ascii=False,allow_nan=False))
shutil.copytree(D,O/'reproducibility/couple_profiles',dirs_exist_ok=True,ignore=shutil.ignore_patterns('__pycache__','*.pyc'))
shutil.copy2(Path(__file__).resolve(),O/'reproducibility/integrate_couple_profiles.py')
print('Integrated joint couple profiles and common-scale contrasts.')
